WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.032 --> 00:00:02.459
Notice that summer seems longer nowadays?

2
00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:08.901
The average temperature has increased over the past one hundred years, but changes in the earth's climate are not new.

3
00:00:08.902 --> 00:00:13.240
The earth's climate has changed constantly over its five-billion-year history.

4
00:00:13.241 --> 00:00:18.578
Sometimes, the climate has warmed up so much that the oceans have risen and covered the earth.

5
00:00:18.579 --> 00:00:24.013
These changes are extreme, but they usually occurred gradually over many thousands of years.

6
00:00:24.014 --> 00:00:31.822
The first modern humans inhabited the earth between fifteen thousand and thirty thousand years ago, when most land was covered in ice.

7
00:00:31.823 --> 00:00:38.497
Fourteen thousand years ago, the last ice sheet began to melt; seven thousand years ago, the ice was gone.

8
00:00:38.498 --> 00:00:41.703
When the "Ice Age" ended, the earth was a different place.

9
00:00:41.704 --> 00:00:44.875
Many kinds of plants and animals had become extinct.

10
00:00:44.876 --> 00:00:50.134
However, the Ice Age was not the last example of extreme climate change.

11
00:00:50.135 --> 00:00:56.538
The Europeans lived through a "Little Ice Age" that began in the fourteenth century and lasted for five hundred years.

12
00:00:56.539 --> 00:01:06.049
During the Little Ice Age, winters were bitterly cold in many parts of the world, but climate changes were most thoroughly documented in Europe and North America.

13
00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:12.904
In the mid-seventeenth century, glaciers in the Swiss Alps grew in size, gradually crushing entire villages.

14
00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:18.625
The River Thames in London and the canals of the Netherlands often froze over during the winter.

15
00:01:18.626 --> 00:01:25.234
Sea ice surrounding Iceland extended for miles in every direction, and ships could no longer dock at its harbors.

16
00:01:25.235 --> 00:01:29.231
There were many years of death and famine due to short crop seasons.

17
00:01:29.232 --> 00:01:35.776
In 1850, the world's climate began warming again and the Little Ice Age came to an end.

18
00:01:35.777 --> 00:01:44.271
Over the last century, the earth has undergone severe climate change again—but this time around, it's not getting colder, it's getting warmer.

19
00:01:44.272 --> 00:01:51.985
The earth has warmed about 1˚Fahrenheit, and the four warmest years of the twentieth century all happened in the 1990s.

20
00:01:51.986 --> 00:01:55.871
This phenomenon is commonly known as "climate change."

21
00:01:55.872 --> 00:02:02.370
Although the earth's climate has drastically altered in the past, the recent climate change is different in two ways.

22
00:02:02.371 --> 00:02:11.975
First, almost all scientists agree that we humans are to blame in some way, and, second, climate change is occurring at an unprecedented pace.

23
00:02:11.976 --> 00:02:17.958
Climate change and its causes, effects, and solutions have become a major issue for all generations.

24
00:02:17.959 --> 00:02:28.108
In a recent survey in Britain, young people said that global warming was their biggest concern for the world's future, even more than the threat of terrorism or lack of housing.

25
00:02:28.109 --> 00:02:35.943
Why is the earth's climate changing? This issue is hotly debated, but most scientists agree that humans are responsible.

26
00:02:35.944 --> 00:02:44.667
The main human activity that negatively affects our climate is the emission of carbon dioxide gas (CO₂) into the earth's atmosphere.

27
00:02:44.668 --> 00:02:57.999
When power plants burn coal, oil, and natural gas to make electricity, they emit CO₂, and when we use a car, bus, or plane, they burn gasoline in their engines and emit CO₂.

28
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.204
Too much CO₂in the atmosphere means that the "greenhouse effect" cannot function normally.

29
00:03:03.205 --> 00:03:09.537
The greenhouse effect is a natural process in which sunlight passes through the atmosphere and warms the earth's surface.

30
00:03:09.538 --> 00:03:13.545
In turn, the land and oceans release heat into the atmosphere.

31
00:03:13.546 --> 00:03:20.189
Water vapor, CO₂, and some other gases absorb some of this heat, and the atmosphere warms up.

32
00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:22.475
The rest of the heat goes into space.

33
00:03:22.476 --> 00:03:29.804
If there is too much CO₂ in the atmosphere, too much heat remains in the atmosphere and so the earth's climate becomes warmer.

34
00:03:29.805 --> 00:03:33.566
And a warmer earth has many consequences.

35
00:03:33.567 --> 00:03:39.024
The effects of climate change are all around us—some are subtle and some are very obvious.

36
00:03:39.025 --> 00:03:51.072
Something that everyone is feeling is hotter weather, called "global warming," with 1998 being the warmest year on record, and 2002 and 2003 tying for second warmest.

37
00:03:51.073 --> 00:03:56.267
In Europe in 2003, 27,000 people died in heat waves.

38
00:03:56.268 --> 00:04:02.554
Eleven thousand Tuvaluans live on nine coral atolls, totaling ten square miles, east of Australia.

39
00:04:02.555 --> 00:04:10.195
Tuvalu has no industry and burns little petroleum, yet this tiny country is on the front line of climate change.

40
00:04:10.196 --> 00:04:17.536
Rising sea levels mean that Tuvalu may be completely submerged and then disappear like the lost city of Atlantis.

41
00:04:17.537 --> 00:04:31.264
In 2002, Prime Minister Koloa Talake announced plans to sue the United States and Australia at the International Court of Justice in the Hague over their disproportionate production of carbon dioxide emissions.

42
00:04:31.265 --> 00:04:37.358
In Antarctica and the Arctic, climate change has reduced the thickness of ice by 40 percent.

43
00:04:37.359 --> 00:04:43.009
Recent changes mean that polar bears may become extinct in this century if weather trends continue.

44
00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:50.111
All kinds of animals and their habits—where they live, what they eat, when they breed—are affected by the warm weather.

45
00:04:50.112 --> 00:04:54.052
Another major consequence of global warming is extreme weather.

46
00:04:54.053 --> 00:04:57.393
Floods and storms are occurring with increasing intensity.

47
00:04:57.394 --> 00:05:10.120
For example, in 2004, Australia had its worst drought in one hundred years and, during severe tropical storms, experienced hailstones the size of tennis balls—the biggest in history.

48
00:05:10.121 --> 00:05:19.139
In Canada, data collected from insurance companies showed that over the past thirty years, more natural catastrophes occurred there than ever before.

49
00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:24.591
With such noticeable changes in the earth's climates, governments have started to take action.

50
00:05:24.592 --> 00:05:33.030
World leaders are slowly acknowledging the existence of climate change and the fact that humans are to blame, but so far, they have been slow to act.

51
00:05:33.031 --> 00:05:37.908
The most famous international treaty on global warming is the Kyoto Protocol.

52
00:05:37.909 --> 00:05:45.463
The 141 nations that signed it in 1998 committed to reducing their country's emissions of CO₂.

53
00:05:45.464 --> 00:05:55.617
Notably, the world's largest producer of CO₂, the United States, as well as Australia, refused to sign the treaty and have received much criticism internationally.

54
00:05:55.618 --> 00:06:01.043
China is the world's second biggest emitter of CO₂ and has signed the Kyoto Protocol.

55
00:06:01.044 --> 00:06:08.622
However, analysts say China faces an uphill battle, with private transport viewed as its major environmental obstacle.

56
00:06:08.623 --> 00:06:13.722
The biggest concern is cars—what will happen when every Chinese citizen drives a car?

57
00:06:13.723 --> 00:06:20.889
What will happen if citizens of developed countries like France and the United States continue to drive cars all the time?

58
00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:30.861
The good news is that we as individuals can reduce climate change by taking responsibility for our part in global warming, and subsequently changing our patterns of energy use.

59
00:06:30.862 --> 00:06:32.694
Here are some suggestions:

60
00:06:32.695 --> 00:06:41.755
1. Tune up your home and make it energy efficient: Heating and cooling our homes, schools, and workplaces use the most energy of all.

61
00:06:41.756 --> 00:06:44.144
Use insulation and ventilation.

62
00:06:44.145 --> 00:06:54.187
2. Buy wisely: if you buy a new appliance or machine like a computer, washing machine, freezer, or fridge, choose one that is energy efficient.

63
00:06:54.188 --> 00:07:00.116
3. Leave the car at home—catch public transport, ride a bike, or walk.

64
00:07:00.117 --> 00:07:07.882
4. Grow your own food—the transport, storage, and cooling of food uses an enormous amount of energy.

65
00:07:07.883 --> 00:07:12.332
As American Senator John Kerry remarked, "Climate change is real.

66
00:07:12.333 --> 00:07:16.401
The longer we wait, the harder the problem will be to solve."

